    <name>
      <text>Estimation-approximation Q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Ahead of a major football match, an event organizer estimates that \({@estimate@}\) fans will attend the stadium, based on ticket sales trends from previous matches.</p>

<p>After the match, the official real count shows that the actual number of fans who attended was \({@actual@}\).</p>

<p>(a) Find the absolute error in the organizer's estimate.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the relative error in the estimate.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Find the percentage error in the estimate.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3> Worked Solution.</h3>
<p>The organizer estimated \({@estimate@}\) fans would attend, while the actual count showed \({@actual@}\) fans attended.</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Absolute error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = |\text{estimate} - \text{actual value}| = |{@estimate@} - {@actual@}| = {@ta1@} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{actual value}} = \frac{{@ta1@}}{{@actual@}} \approx {@ta2@} \]</p>

<p>(c) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta2@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta3@}\% \]</p>

<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> Estimating attendance ahead of time helps organizers plan staffing, security, and seating. A large percentage error could mean the venue is understaffed or overstaffed compared to what's actually needed on the day.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
